{
  "abstract": "Prime values of positive definite binary quadratic forms are often bounded by all primitive values, losing information about composite norms. We construct explicit finite subtractions using the projective zero directions modulo known split primes. A common inner region supplies lower masses, while an outer region bounds the full sum using the same weight. For two primes, a joint directional histogram accounts for overlaps exactly: after choosing one root pair, the remaining minimization selects the two least costly residual directions. The leading coefficient further determines whether infinity is a permitted or required root.\n\nFor an imported quadratic determinant argument, we construct a monotone switching-loss surrogate with rational parameters and prove its connection to the published row and rank formulas. Exact geometric boxes and directed logarithm arithmetic enclose the masses for $56{,}000{,}000\\le D\\le64{,}000{,}000$. A complete application with class number 30 and two ramified primes covers all 20,988 seed cases. With identical geometry and ramification partition, the loss bound is below $0.771021320$ without subtraction, $0.765909099$ with two-prime unions, and $0.761163973$ with the leading-coefficient root restriction.\n\nA fifteen-stage CPU replay supplies exact scientific fingerprints. Exhaustive array checks and actual quadratic forms test the finite optimizations. The determinant framework, class-capacity constraints, analytic input tables and bounds outside the stated interval remain explicit imports. The contribution is the common-weight construction and complete bounded-interval application; no new discriminant endpoint, field census or class-number-one improvement is claimed. The bounded comparison does not use the infinite-tail radius estimate questioned in a separate author audit.",
